problem:
Three different one-digit positive integers are placed in the bottom row of cells. Numbers in adjacent cells are added and the sum is placed in the cell above them. In the second row, continue the same process to obtain a number in the top cell. What is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers possible in the top cell? [asy]
path box=(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle;
draw(box);
draw(shift(1.5,0)*box);
draw(shift(3,0)*box);
draw(shift(.75,2)*box);
draw(shift(2.25,2)*box);
draw(shift(1.5,4)*box);
picture p;
draw(p, (.6,.2)--(.95, .8), EndArrow);
draw(p, (1.9,.2)--(1.55, .8), EndArrow);
draw(p, (1.25, .8)--(1.25,.6));
draw(p, (1.15, .7)--(1.35,.7));
add(shift(0,1)*p);
add(shift(1.5,1)*p);
add(shift(.75,3)*p);
[/asy]

In the figure below, $ABDC,$ $EFHG,$ and $ASHY$ are all squares; $AB=EF =1$ and $AY=5$.

What is the area of quadrilateral $DYES$?

[asy]
size(5cm);
defaultpen(black+1);
pair a=(0,5); pair b=(1,5); pair c=(0,4); pair d=(1,4); pair e=(4,1); pair f=(5,1); pair g=(4,0); pair h=(5,0); pair y=(0,0); pair s=(5,5);
draw(a--s--h--y--a);
draw(c--d--b,gray);
draw(g--e--f,gray);
draw(d--y--e--s--d);
dot(a); dot(b); dot(c); dot(d); dot(e); dot(f); dot(g); dot(h); dot(y); dot(s);
label("$A$",a,NW);
label("$B$",b,N);
label("$C$",c,W);
label("$D$",d,SE);
label("$E$",e,NW);
label("$F$",f,E);
label("$G$",g,S);
label("$H$",h,SE);
label("$Y$",y,SW);
label("$S$",s,NE);
[/asy]

In the arrow-shaped polygon shown below, the angles at vertices $A$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F$ are right angles, $BC = FG = 5$, $CD = FE = 20$, $DE = 10$, and $AB = AG$.  Find the area of polygon $ABCDEFG$.

[asy]
unitsize(1.5 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, E, F, G;

A = (0,0);
B = (1,1);
C = (1,0.5);
D = (2.5,0.5);
E = (2.5,-0.5);
F = (1,-0.5);
G = (1,-1);

draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--G--cycle);

label("$A$", A, W);
label("$B$", B, NE);
label("$C$", C, S);
label("$D$", D, NE);
label("$E$", E, SE);
label("$F$", F, N);
label("$G$", G, SE);
label("$5$", (B + C)/2, dir(0));
label("$20$", (C + D)/2, N);
label("$10$", (D + E)/2, dir(0));
label("$20$", (E + F)/2, S);
label("$5$", (F + G)/2, dir(0));
[/asy]

The length of the diameter of this spherical ball is equal to the height of the box in which it is placed. The box is a cube and has an edge length of 30 cm. How many cubic centimeters of the box are not occupied by the solid sphere? Express your answer in terms of $\pi$. [asy]
draw(Circle((7.5,7.5),5));
draw((7.5,6.2)..(2.5,7.5)--(12.5,7.5)..cycle);
draw((7.5,8.8)..(2.5,7.5)--(12.5,7.5)..cycle,linetype("2 4"));
fill((2.6,7.4)--(2.6,7.6)--(12.4,7.6)--(12.4,7.4)--cycle,white);
dot((7.5,7.5));
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle);
draw((0,10)--(5,15)--(15,15)--(10,10));
draw((15,15)--(15,5)--(10,0));
draw((0,0)--(5,5)--(15,5),dashed); draw((5,5)--(5,15),dashed);
[/asy]

The figure below is composed of the rectangles $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ with whole number values for length and width. The areas of regions $A$, $B$, and $C$ in square meters, are shown in the diagram. What is the area in square meters of rectangle $D$? [asy]
draw((0,0)--(0,11)--(13,11)--(13,0)--cycle);
draw((0,6)--(13,6)); draw((8,11)--(8,0));
